# Expectation record for 04_bitfields/006_in_union_and_nested.c
#
# Reproduce one cell by hand with no harness: render the three command templates
# below, substituting the target triple, the optimization level, the source path,
# the output path, and the target's runner (empty on the natively executing target).

program            = 006_in_union_and_nested
area               = 04_bitfields
description        = Bitfields declared inside unions and inside nested aggregates, with same-member union read-back, folded and volatile runtime variants
targets            = x86_64, i686, aarch64, riscv64
opt_levels         = -O0, -O1, -O2
shared_flags       = -static
bcc_command        = $BCC --target <triple> <opt> -static <src> -o <out>
ref_command        = $REF_CC_<TRIPLE> <opt> -static <src> -o <out>
run_command        = <runner> <out>
expect_exit        = 0
oracle_a           = enabled
oracle_b           = enabled
oracle_c           = enabled
ub_notes           <<END
The default undefined-behaviour warning gate applies unmodified, with no deviation:
-Wall -Wextra -pedantic -Wconversion -Wsign-conversion -Wshadow -Werror.  The program
was verified to pass that gate and to run cleanly under
-fsanitize=undefined,address -fno-sanitize-recover=all.

Every union access reads back the member that was last written, and this is the program's
central safety property.  Reading a union member other than the one last written would yield an
unspecified value, so a divergence there would prove nothing about either compiler.  The union
of three views has each view written completely and then read on its own, and the views are
never mixed; the union of two single bitfields is written and read one member at a time; the
union nested inside a struct has its flat member written and read, and then both members of its
pair sub-struct written before either is read.  No read anywhere in the program follows a write
to a different member.

No padding is observed anywhere.  Several of these aggregates necessarily contain padding bits
or padding bytes, whose values are unspecified even after every named field has been assigned,
so the program prints no byte image at all and reads only named members.

Every store is a constant inside the destination field's representable range, so no conversion
can change a value.  No arithmetic is performed on any bitfield, so no signed overflow can
occur, and no shift operator appears anywhere in the program.  No aliasing violation: no pointer
is formed to any bitfield and no type-punning of any kind occurs.  No uninitialized read: every
object has static storage duration, the one with nested aggregates carries an explicit constant
initializer and the rest are zero-initialized, and in every case each member is written before
it is read.  No object is modified twice between sequence points.  No pointer value, address,
timestamp, random value or locale-dependent format is printed.

Every property is computed twice, once from literal constants and once from volatile-qualified
storage, so the constant folder's answer cannot stand in for the one the backend computes at run
time.

No header is included and printf is declared by hand, so nothing here depends on a
standard-library header bcc does not ship.
END
impl_defined_notes <<END
Every bitfield is declared with an explicit signed int or unsigned int base type, at every
nesting level, including the members of the sub-struct inside the nested union.  A plain int
bitfield's signedness is implementation-defined, so using it would manufacture a spurious
divergence; stating the signedness in the declaration removes that dependency entirely.

Plain char is not used.  Plain char signedness was measured to differ across the four targets
(signed on x86_64 and i686, unsigned on aarch64 and riscv64), so it is avoided entirely.

No value whose width varies by target is printed.  sizeof(long) and sizeof(void *) differ on
i686 (4) from the other three targets (8), so neither is printed, and no pointer value or
address appears in the output.  Every bitfield is declared on an int base type, and int is four
bytes on all four targets, which removes one variable; the allocation unit a bitfield sequence
occupies is still implementation-defined, so the printed sizes and alignments are measured
expectations for the four ABIs under test -- the inner struct four bytes, the outer struct
twenty, the tagged struct twelve and both unions four -- rather than values derived from the
base type.  Every bitfield read is cast explicitly to unsigned int or int before it is
passed to printf, and the one non-bitfield value printed, the whole-word view of the first
union, is already an unsigned int of a width the format specifier matches on every target.

The distinctive assertion of this program is non-disturbance across nesting levels.  Four of the
eight members of the nested aggregate are modified and the other four are asserted unchanged, and
one three-bit field inside a union inside a struct is modified with its three neighbours asserted
unchanged.  A bitfield insert that read, modified and wrote back too wide a storage unit would
change one of those neighbours and be caught here.

All four targets and all three oracles remain enabled.  Bitfield layout, size, alignment and
read-back values were measured identical on all four targets, so a cross-backend divergence here
is a genuine finding rather than an implementation-defined difference.
END
